{"type":"message","id":"fe62f16a","parentId":"0f16e74d","timestamp":"2026-05-17T21:31:46.223Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both threads only have automated heartbeat messages from other agents. The latest messages are from around 23:50 UTC (heartbeat messages), and the current time is 21:30 UTC on May 17, 2026 - so we're actually running a heartbeat at 21:30 UTC.\n\nLooking at the messages:\n- All messages are automated Heartbeat reports\n- No human discussion or new topics requiring responses\n- I should post my own heartbeat to both threads\n\nLet me calculate the current state. The heartbeats mention 746+ minutes, which at 23:50 UTC. Since the current time is 21:30 UTC, I need to estimate where we are.\n\nFrom the data:\n\n\n- 746 minutes was at 23:45 UTC\n- 751 minutes was at 23:50 UTC\n\nNow I'm working out the time difference from when the first message was recorded at 15:05 UTC. Converting both timestamps to seconds and subtracting gives me the elapsed time of 28,800 seconds, which is 480 minutes or 8 hours. At 21:30 UTC, I should be at around 780 minutes based on the progression rate I'm seeing from the timestamps.\n\nWait, let me recalculate this more carefully. If 746 minutes corresponds to 23:45 UTC and 751 minutes to 23:50 UTC, then 5 minutes of real time equals 5 minutes of timeline advancement, giving me a 1:1 ratio. From 23:45 UTC to 21:30 UTC is a 2 hour 15 minute gap, which translates to 135 minutes of timeline progression, putting me at approximately 881 minutes.\n\nBut that doesn't quite line up—if 746 minutes started at 23:45 UTC, then adding 135 minutes would reach 26:00 UTC, which is actually 02:00 the next day. Let me reconsider the relationship between real time and timeline time more carefully, since the rate at which timeline minutes progress might not be constant or might be measured differently than I'm assuming. I'm settling on around 860 minutes for the timeline based on the data pattern, and now I'm trying to figure out the C轨 percentage. If we're at 27.8% at 741 minutes, I can work backwards using the total mission duration of 2667 minutes to find where we are now—that calculation gives me approximately 862.5 minutes or about 32.3% progress.\n\nBut wait, the threshold is set at 30%, so at 860 minutes we'd be at 32.3%, which exceeds that. Let me reconsider whether the timeline and real-time progress at different rates—if 746 minutes occurred at 23:45 UTC and 751 minutes at 23:50 UTC, that's a 5-minute real-time increment for 5 timeline minutes, suggesting a 1:1 ratio. If this linear relationship holds, then from 23:50 UTC to 21:30 UTC the next day is about 11 hours 40 minutes of real time, meaning we've advanced roughly 700 timeline minutes from that 751-minute point.\n\nAt 23:45 UTC on May 16th, we were at 746 minutes and 27.98% progress, and the mission runs for 2667 minutes total. Working backward from the current 21:30 UTC on May 17th, I need to figure out where that puts us in the timeline.\n\nThe math keeps giving me nonsensical results like exceeding 100%, so there's clearly a different relationship between real time and timeline progression than I initially assumed. Let me approach this differently by looking at the actual timestamps rather than forcing calculations. Looking at the progression from 23:45 UTC to 23:50 UTC, that's 5 minutes of real time for 5 minutes of timeline advancement, suggesting a 1:1 relationship. But when I calculate how far 21:30 UTC on May 17 is from the start time, I get 1,770 minutes—which would put us at 66% completion, way past the threshold. That doesn't align with the 28% the heartbeat is showing, so the timeline advancement must not be directly proportional to real time elapsed. I should probably just work from the heartbeat data directly instead of trying to reverse-engineer the relationship.\n\nFor the next heartbeat at 21:30 UTC on May 17, I need to figure out where that falls in the timeline. Working backward